// @no-instantiate expected i32
// @hint redundant type annotation: `xs` is inferred as `(boolean | null)[]` — remove it to lean on inference
// ACCEPTS, THEN MISCOMPILES. A nullable-ELEMENT list ANNOTATION whose initializer is a CALL
// rather than an array literal: `vl check --codegen` exits 0 and the emitted module does not
// validate — `type mismatch: expected i32, found (ref $type)`.
//
// AND THE COMPILER HINTS YOU TO DELETE THE ANNOTATION THAT BREAKS IT. The `@hint` above is
// real: the linter calls `xs`'s annotation redundant because inference reaches the same type.
// Taking that advice makes the program compile — so on this shape the hint is, accidentally, a
// workaround for a codegen bug, and writing the type you meant is what costs you a valid module.
// A "redundant" annotation that changes codegen is not redundant; that the two ever disagree is
// worth its own look when this is fixed.
//
// THE ANNOTATION IS LOAD-BEARING, and the matrix says which one. Annotating the INNER binding
// changes nothing; annotating the OUTER one is what produces the bad module. With NO outer
// annotation the program fails differently and LOUDLY (`emitProgram: field access but no struct
// type declared`) — a separate gap in inferring a nullable-element list RETURN, not this one.
//
// TWO CONTROLS in `lists/nullable-elem-list-call-init-controls.vl` place it precisely: the same
// annotation with a direct array-LITERAL init compiles and runs, and the same call init with a
// NON-nullable element type compiles and runs. Neither half is broken alone; the pair is. The
// litunion spelling (`type K = "a" | "b"`, `const xs: (K | null)[] = mk()`) fails identically, so
// this is the nullable-element REP and not booleans.
//
// HOW IT WAS FOUND, because the route says something about the corpus. `letIsArray`'s
// `nameIsI32Array(lt.tyName)` rung sat on a re-measurement queue — a name test whose arena
// sibling `exprArray` claims every case, so sabotaging it moves 0 corpus rows. A census asked
// what the corpus was MISSING and answered: it carries `(boolean|null)[]` and `(K|null)[]`
// annotations in quantity — 14 files and 41 files — and **every one of them initializes from an
// array LITERAL**, which `exprArray` claims on the line below. Not one uses a call.
//
// So the shape written to PIN a redundant arm was a live miscompile instead. The arm's 0 really
// is redundancy, exactly as the queue said; what no sweep could show is that the redundancy sat
// one construction away from every fixture in the corpus.
function mk() {
  const ys = [true, null]
  return ys
}

const xs: (boolean | null)[] = mk()
print(xs.length)
